 '70 to 150 kV depending on scanner model; many scanners offer 80, 100, 120 and 140 kV. The IAEA handbook gives 120 to 140 kV as usual, with 80 to 100 kV for contrast studies and children.',
 [IAEA, FORCE, VISION, NAEOTOM],
 'Draft confirmed. Read: IAEA Diagnostic Radiology Physics handbook (2014), section 11.6 ("a relatively high tube voltage (120-140 kV) ... low tube voltage, in the range 80-100 kV") and section on dosimetry beam qualities (the standard CT quality RQT 9 is 120 kV). Siemens SOMATOM Force specification: "70 - 150 kV, in steps of 10 kV". Siemens Biograph Vision PET/CT: tube voltages 70, 80, 100, 120, 140 kV, so the CT inside a PET/CT may top out at 140 kV. Not every scanner offers 70 or 150 kV. "kVp" and "kV" are used interchangeably in the sources.');
C('ct-beam-energy-spectrum', 'Claim 1: X-ray beam energy spectrum and effective energy',
 'The beam is a mix of photon energies up to the tube voltage (up to 120 keV at 120 kV), and it behaves on average like a single-energy beam of roughly 55 to 70 keV.',
 '55 to 70', 'keV (effective energy at 120 kV)',
 'Measured effective energy at 120 kV, in air at the scanner center: 54.4 and 62.4 keV on two scanners (one study), 54.9 to 58.0 keV on two other scanners (another study). PET/CT papers use about 70 keV for the beam inside the patient.',
 [EFFE1, EFFE2, KINAHAN98, KINAHAN03, TOWNSEND04],
 'CORRECTED from the draft "about 60 to 70 keV": measured effective energies at 120 kVp are often below 60 keV (54.4 keV and 62.4 keV in PMC5966206; 54.87 to 57.97 keV, mean 56.47, in PMC7851788). The PET/CT literature (Kinahan 1998 abstract: "effective CT photon energy of 70 keV"; Kinahan 2003: "approximately 70 keV"; Townsend 2004: "mean photon energy of 70 keV") uses 70 keV; the beam gets "harder" (higher average energy) as low-energy photons are filtered out by the patient. "Effective energy" (single energy with the same half-value layer) and "mean energy" (average of the spectrum) are not the same quantity; sources use both loosely. Kinahan 1998 describes the CT photons as spanning 40 keV through 140 keV. The site tables use 70 keV to match the PET/CT convention; say "about 70 keV" rather than implying it is exact.');
// 2
C('beer-lambert-law', 'Claim 2: Beer-Lambert law',
 'The number of photons that get straight through a material falls off exponentially with thickness: I = I0 x exp(-mu x x), where mu is the linear attenuation coefficient of the material and x is the thickness.',
 'I = I0 * exp(-mu * x)', '',
 'Exact only for a narrow beam of a single energy passing through one uniform material; for a path through several tissues the exponent is the sum (integral) of mu along the path.',
 [IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed. IAEA handbook Eq. 11.1 ("Beer\'s law": I(x) = I0 e^(-mu x)) and Eq. 11.2 for a path through varying tissue. The handbook notes that the law describes only the primary (unscattered) beam and that a real CT beam has many energies, which is the cause of the beam-hardening artifact. The site may call it Beer\'s law, the Beer-Lambert law or the exponential attenuation law; all are in use.');
// 3
C('water-attenuation-70-and-511-kev', 'Claim 3: attenuation table; water at 70 keV and 511 keV',
 'Water stops about ' + Math.round(100*(1-Math.exp(-w.mu70))) + ' percent of 70 keV X-ray photons per centimeter (mu about ' + sig(w.mu70,2) + ' per cm) but only about ' + Math.round(100*(1-Math.exp(-w.mu511))) + ' percent of the 511 keV photons used in PET (mu about 0.096 per cm).',
 'mu(water) = ' + sig(w.mu70) + ' per cm at 70 keV (log-log interpolation; ' + sig(w.mu70x) + ' from NIST XCOM run at exactly 70 keV); ' + sig(w.mu511) + ' per cm at 511 keV', 'per cm',
 'Half-value thickness of water: about ' + n.hvlW70 + ' cm at 70 keV and about ' + n.hvlW511 + ' cm at 511 keV. PET papers quote 0.096 per cm (narrow beam) and 0.093 to 0.095 per cm as measured on scanners that accept some scatter.',
 [NIST+'water.html', TAB4, XCOM_COMP, BURGER02, CARNEY06, BAI03],
 'Draft value 0.096 per cm CONFIRMED. NIST table values for water: 0.2059 (60 keV), 0.1837 (80 keV), 0.09687 (500 keV), 0.08956 (600 keV) cm2/g. Log-log interpolation gives ' + sig(w.mr70) + ' cm2/g at 70 keV and ' + sig(w.mr511) + ' cm2/g at 511 keV; NIST XCOM run at exactly those energies gives 0.1929 and 0.09598 cm2/g. Independent PET sources: Burger et al. 2002 (Eur J Nucl Med Mol Imaging 29:922-7, PMID 12111133) abstract: "the narrow-beam attenuation coefficient of water at 511 keV (0.096 cm-1)", and they measured 0.093 cm-1 from germanium-68 transmission scans; Carney et al. 2006 (Med Phys 33:976-83, PMID 16696474) use 9.6e-5 x (HU+1000), i.e. 0.096 per cm at 0 HU; Bai et al. 2003 (J Nucl Med 44:1855-61) use 0.096 cm-1 for soft tissue. PubMed abstracts were read through the NCBI E-utilities URL listed, because pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov returned a bot check to curl.');
C('attenuation-table-materials', 'Claim 3: attenuation table for seven materials',
 'At CT energies, one centimeter of dense bone removes about ' + Math.round(100*(1-Math.exp(-b.mu70))) + ' percent of the X-ray photons, water or muscle about ' + Math.round(100*(1-Math.exp(-w.mu70))) + ' percent, fat about ' + Math.round(100*(1-Math.exp(-f.mu70))) + ' percent, air-filled lung about ' + Math.round(100*(1-Math.exp(-l.mu70))) + ' percent, and air almost none.',
 'mu at 70 keV (per cm): ' + M.map(m => m.key + ' ' + sig(m.mu70)).join('; ') + '. mu at 511 keV (per cm): ' + M.map(m => m.key + ' ' + sig(m.mu511)).join('; '), 'per cm',
 'See tables.attenuation. Body tissues vary from person to person; lung varies most (roughly 0.1 to 0.5 g/cm3 with breathing), and bone ranges from spongy to compact.',
 [TAB4, TAB2, ...M.map(m => NIST+m.page), XCOM_MIX],
 'Mass attenuation coefficients at 60, 80, 500 and 600 keV were read from the NIST pages and densities from NIST Table 2 (air 1.205E-03, adipose 0.950, water 1.000, soft tissue ICRU-44 1.060, skeletal muscle 1.050, cortical bone 1.920, lung tissue 1.050 g/cm3). mu at 70 and 511 keV by log-log interpolation as the task specified. CHECK: NIST XCOM run directly at 70 keV with the same compositions gives slightly lower values, because the curve between 60 and 80 keV is not a straight line on log-log axes: water 0.1929 vs 0.1937 cm2/g (0.4% lower), soft tissue 0.1916 vs 0.1924, fat 0.1875 vs 0.1879, cortical bone 0.2570 vs 0.2616 (1.8% lower). The XCOM values are in the table as xcom_* columns and are the better numbers if the site shows more than two significant figures; at 511 keV the two methods agree to 4 figures. DOUBT: the inflated-lung row uses the NIST lung-tissue mu/rho (tabulated for solid lung tissue, 1.05 g/cm3) at an assumed density of 0.26 g/cm3, as the task instructed. 0.26 g/cm3 is the inflated-lung value usually attributed to ICRU Report 44, but I could not open a primary source that states it; the site should say "inflated lung, assumed density 0.26 g/cm3". The computed HU for that row (' + Math.round(l.hu70) + ') sits inside the IAEA lung range (-950 to -600). typicalHU ranges are from claim 5 sources, not from NIST. computedHU_monoenergetic shows what a single-energy beam would give; real scanners differ.');
C('bone-vs-water-attenuation-ratio', 'Claim 3: attenuation table; bone compared with water at the two energies',
 'Dense bone attenuates 70 keV X-rays about 2.6 times as strongly as water, but 511 keV PET photons only about 1.8 times as strongly.',
 'mu(bone)/mu(water) = ' + n.ratioBW70 + ' at 70 keV (' + n.ratioBW70x + ' with XCOM values); ' + n.ratioBW511 + ' at 511 keV', 'ratio',
 'For ICRU-44 cortical bone at 1.92 g/cm3; less for spongy bone.',
 [NIST+'bone.html', NIST+'water.html', XCOM_MIX, TOWNSEND04],
 'Computed: bone ' + sig(b.mu70) + ' per cm and water ' + sig(w.mu70) + ' per cm at 70 keV; bone ' + sig(b.mu511) + ' and water ' + sig(w.mu511) + ' at 511 keV. This energy dependence is the reason one scale factor cannot convert a CT image to 511 keV: the ratio mu(511)/mu(70) is ' + n.scaleWater + ' for water and ' + n.scaleBone + ' for cortical bone (XCOM values). Townsend et al. 2004 state the same point in words: scale factors for bone are smaller than for soft tissue.');
// 4
C('transmission-through-water-70-kev', 'Claim 4: percentage of 70 keV photons passing straight through',
 'Of 100 X-ray photons at 70 keV aimed into water, about ' + Math.round(tr('water').pctThrough_1cm) + ' pass straight through 1 cm, ' + Math.round(tr('water').pctThrough_5cm) + ' through 5 cm, ' + Math.round(tr('water').pctThrough_10cm) + ' through 10 cm and only ' + Math.round(tr('water').pctThrough_20cm) + ' through 20 cm.',
 [1,5,10,20].map((x,i) => x + ' cm: ' + [tr('water').pctThrough_1cm,tr('water').pctThrough_5cm,tr('water').pctThrough_10cm,tr('water').pctThrough_20cm][i] + '%').join('; '), 'percent',
 'Muscle and other soft tissue are within about 2 percentage points of water. See tables.transmission for all seven materials.',
 [NIST+'water.html', IAEA],
 'Calculation: percent = 100 x exp(-mu x thickness) with mu = ' + sig(w.mu70) + ' per cm. 1 cm: 100 x exp(-' + sig(w.mu70) + ') = ' + tr('water').pctThrough_1cm + '. 5 cm: ' + tr('water').pctThrough_5cm + '. 10 cm: ' + tr('water').pctThrough_10cm + '. 20 cm: ' + tr('water').pctThrough_20cm + '. With the XCOM value (0.1929 per cm) the results are ' + JSON.stringify(tr('water').xcomCheck_pctThrough) + ', the same after rounding to whole percent except 20 cm (2.1%). "Straight through" means not absorbed and not scattered, for an ideal narrow single-energy beam; a real CT beam has a spread of energies and its average energy rises with depth, so real transmission through thick bodies is somewhat higher than these numbers. The script is scripts/build_draft.js in the batch scratch folder.');
C('transmission-through-bone-and-lung-70-kev', 'Claim 4: percentage of 70 keV photons passing straight through',
 'About ' + Math.round(tr('bone').pctThrough_1cm) + ' percent of 70 keV photons pass straight through 1 cm of dense bone and about ' + Math.round(tr('bone').pctThrough_5cm) + ' percent through 5 cm, while about ' + Math.round(tr('lung').pctThrough_10cm) + ' percent pass through 10 cm of air-filled lung.',
 'bone: ' + [tr('bone').pctThrough_1cm,tr('bone').pctThrough_5cm,tr('bone').pctThrough_10cm,tr('bone').pctThrough_20cm].join(' / ') + ' %; inflated lung: ' + [tr('lung').pctThrough_1cm,tr('lung').pctThrough_5cm,tr('lung').pctThrough_10cm,tr('lung').pctThrough_20cm].join(' / ') + ' %; fat: ' + [tr('adipose').pctThrough_1cm,tr('adipose').pctThrough_5cm,tr('adipose').pctThrough_10cm,tr('adipose').pctThrough_20cm].join(' / ') + ' %; air: ' + [tr('air').pctThrough_1cm,tr('air').pctThrough_5cm,tr('air').pctThrough_10cm,tr('air').pctThrough_20cm].join(' / ') + ' % (1 / 5 / 10 / 20 cm)', 'percent',
 'Bone values are for compact (cortical) bone; no part of the body has 10 or 20 cm of solid cortical bone, so those two entries are illustrative only. Lung depends on how full of air it is.',
 [NIST+'bone.html', NIST+'lung.html', NIST+'adipose.html', NIST+'air.html'],
 'Calculation as for water, with mu from tables.attenuation (bone ' + sig(b.mu70) + ', lung ' + sig(l.mu70) + ', fat ' + sig(f.mu70) + ', air ' + sig(a.mu70) + ' per cm). CAUTION on thick bone: small differences in mu are magnified by the exponential. With the XCOM mu for bone (' + sig(b.mu70x) + ' per cm) the results are ' + JSON.stringify(tr('bone').xcomCheck_pctThrough) + ' percent, so for 10 cm say "less than 1 percent" and for 20 cm say "less than 0.01 percent" rather than quoting digits.');
// 5
C('hounsfield-unit-definition', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield unit definition',
 'CT images are stored in Hounsfield units, which compare each tissue with water: HU = 1000 x (mu - mu_water) / mu_water, so 1 HU is a 0.1 percent difference from water.',
 'HU = 1000 * (mu - mu_water) / mu_water', 'HU',
 'Some texts write the denominator as (mu_water - mu_air), which fixes air at exactly -1000; because air attenuates about 900 times less than water the two forms differ by about 1 HU at the air end and not at all at water.',
 [IAEA, HOUNSFIELD],
 'Draft formula kept as an accepted variant, but the form with mu_water alone in the denominator is the one given by the IAEA handbook (Eq. 11.4), which then states HU(air) = -1000 by taking mu_air = 0. With the NIST values at 70 keV the IAEA form gives air = ' + n.airHU_iaeaForm + ' HU, so "air is -1000" is a rounded convention under that form and exact under the two-point form. Both forms are true to within 1.1 HU. The scale is named for Godfrey Hounsfield; in his 1979 Nobel lecture he describes the scale running "from air (-1000) at the bottom ... to bone at the top", with water chosen as zero.');
C('hounsfield-air-and-water', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, fixed points',
 'On the Hounsfield scale water is 0 and air is -1000.',
 'water 0; air -1000', 'HU',
 'Fixed by definition and by scanner calibration; all other tissues shift a little with tube voltage, scanner model and reconstruction settings.',
 [IAEA, HOUNSFIELD],
 'Draft confirmed. IAEA handbook section 11.2.2 and Table 11.1; Hounsfield Nobel lecture ("air (-1000) ... water, which has been chosen to be zero"). The IAEA handbook notes a 12-bit CT image covers -1024 to +3071 HU.');
C('hounsfield-lung', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, lung',
 'Air-filled lung measures about -950 to -600 HU, because it is mostly air.',
 '-750', 'HU', '-950 to -600 (IAEA); more negative at full breath-in, less negative at breath-out or where the lung is diseased or collapsed',
 [IAEA, NIST+'lung.html'],
 'Draft range (-900 to -500) replaced with the IAEA handbook Table 11.1 entry: Lung -750 (-950 to -600). The computed value for lung tissue at 0.26 g/cm3 and 70 keV is ' + Math.round(l.hu70) + ' HU, consistent with the table. The draft upper end of -500 is plausible for lung at breath-out but I did not find it in a source I could read.');
C('hounsfield-fat', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, fat',
 'Fat measures about -100 HU, slightly below water, typically between -130 and -70 HU.',
 '-100', 'HU', '-130 to -70. IAEA: -90 (-100 to -80). Measured in 48 adults on two scanners: mean -103 to -113, individual values -130 to -79.',
 [IAEA, LAMBA, HU1980],
 'Draft range (-120 to -60) adjusted: no source read supports values as high as -60 for pure fat. IAEA Table 11.1: Fat -90 (-100 to -80). Lamba et al. 2014 (AJR 203:1013-20), Table 2: subcutaneous fat means -102.9 to -113.4 HU, minimum -129.9, maximum -78.9. A 2018 paper quoting Hounsfield (1980) gives adipose -90 to -70. The same person measured on two scanners differed by about 10 HU in fat.');
C('hounsfield-blood', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, blood',
 'Blood without contrast agent measures roughly +30 to +60 HU, a little above water.',
 '+40 to +55', 'HU', '+30 to +60; lower in anemia (measured down to +20), higher when clotted',
 [IAEA, AORTA1, AORTA2],
 'Draft range (+30 to +45) WIDENED because sources disagree. IAEA Table 11.1: Blood +55 (+50 to +60). Measured in the aorta on unenhanced CT: mean +37.7 HU, range +20 to +51, in 423 patients of whom about a third were anemic (PMC9816921); about +45 HU in non-anemic and +33 to +36 HU in anemic patients (PMC12426566). The two clinical studies are single-center studies in journals of modest standing; treat the numbers as typical, not exact.');
C('hounsfield-muscle', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, muscle',
 'Muscle measures roughly +10 to +65 HU, most often about +40 to +50 HU.',
 '+45', 'HU', '+10 to +65. IAEA: +25 (+10 to +40). Measured psoas muscle in 47 adults on two scanners: mean +44 to +49, individual values +22 to +67.',
 [IAEA, LAMBA, NIST+'muscle.html'],
 'Draft range (+35 to +55) widened because sources disagree. IAEA Table 11.1: Muscle +25 (+10 to +40). Lamba et al. 2014 Table 2: right and left psoas means 44.4, 44.0, 47.7, 49.4 HU; minimum 21.9, maximum 66.6. Computed for ICRU-44 skeletal muscle at 70 keV: +' + Math.round(mu_.hu70) + ' HU. Fat within a muscle lowers its value.');
C('hounsfield-liver', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, liver',
 'A healthy liver measures about +45 to +70 HU without contrast agent, and fat in the liver lowers the number.',
 '+55', 'HU', '+45 to +70. IAEA: +60 (+50 to +70). Measured in 45 adult patients on two scanners: mean +47.5 and +50.4.',
 [IAEA, LAMBA],
 'Draft range (+40 to +60) shifted slightly upward to match the sources read. IAEA Table 11.1: Liver +60 (+50 to +70). Lamba et al. 2014 Table 2: right lobe mean 47.5 HU (Siemens) and 50.4 HU (GE), individual values 9.6 to 77.2 HU in hospital patients not screened for fatty liver, which pulls the mean down.');
C('hounsfield-spongy-bone', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, spongy (cancellous) bone',
 'Spongy bone, such as the inside of a vertebra, varies widely with age and bone health, from under +100 HU to +300 HU or more.',
 '+100 to +300', 'HU', 'In a study of 1867 adults, a first-lumbar-vertebra reading of 160 HU or less suggested osteoporosis and readings above 200 HU made it very unlikely; individual readings as low as 20 HU were shown.',
 [PICKHARDT, IAEA],
 'CORRECTED: the draft range "+300 to +400" is widely repeated on the web but I found no primary source for it, and measured vertebral spongy bone is usually lower. Pickhardt et al. 2013 (Ann Intern Med; PMC3736840): L1 trabecular attenuation of 160 HU or less was 90% sensitive, and 110 HU more than 90% specific, for osteoporosis; negative predictive value 99% above 200 HU; example patients at 20, 93 and 148 HU. Spongy bone in young adults and at other sites can be denser; the IAEA table gives only "compact bone" starting at +300. Recommend the site avoid a single narrow range for spongy bone.');
C('hounsfield-cortical-bone', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, compact (cortical) bone',
 'Dense compact bone measures from several hundred HU to +2000 HU or more, typically around +1000 to +1500 HU.',
 '+1000', 'HU', '+300 to +2500 (IAEA). Depends strongly on tube voltage: computed for reference cortical bone, +' + Math.round(b.hu80) + ' at 80 keV, +' + Math.round(b.hu70) + ' at 70 keV and +' + Math.round(b.hu60) + ' at 60 keV.',
 [IAEA, HEADPH, NIST+'bone.html'],
 'Draft (+500 up to about +1900 or more) is consistent with the sources; range replaced with the IAEA handbook Table 11.1 entry: Compact bone +1000 (+300 to +2500). A 2021 phantom paper lists skull bone at about 1500 HU at 120 kV on a GE Revolution scanner. The IAEA handbook notes that HU of high-atomic-number materials such as bone change most with tube voltage.');
// 6
C('ct-gantry-rotation-time', 'Claim 6: gantry rotation speed',
 'Inside the ring, the X-ray tube and the detector opposite it spin around the patient about two to four times every second.',
 '0.25 to 0.5', 's per rotation',
 'Fastest current models: 0.23 s (GE Revolution Apex), 0.24 s (Canon Aquilion ONE INSIGHT Edition, optional), 0.25 s (Siemens SOMATOM Force). The CT inside a Siemens Biograph Vision PET/CT: 0.33 s, with 0.30 and 0.28 s optional. Slower settings of 0.5 to 1 s are also used.',
 [IAEA, FORCE, GEPR, FDA_GE, CANON, VISION],
 'Draft confirmed with detail added. 0.5 s = 2 turns per second; 0.33 s = 3; 0.25 s = 4; 0.23 s = ' + n.rps['0.23'] + '. IAEA handbook: multi-detector scanners brought rotation time to 0.3-0.4 s, and "forces of several tens of g arise for rotation times of the order of 0.25 s". Manufacturer pages read 2026-11-18; GE press release calls 0.23 s "the world\'s fastest gantry speed", a marketing claim the site should not repeat as fact. The FDA 510(k) summary K213715 confirms the 0.23 s/rotation option. The fastest speeds are options used mainly for heart scans; a PET/CT body scan does not need them.');
C('ct-detector-rows', 'Claim 6: number of detector rows',
 'Instead of one row of detector elements, a modern CT scanner has many rows side by side, commonly 64 and up to 320, so each turn records a band of the body rather than a single thin slice.',
 '64', 'detector rows (common); up to 320',
 '4 to 64 rows in the multi-detector scanners introduced from 1998; up to 320 rows of 0.5 mm, covering 16 cm per turn, on wide-detector scanners. The CT inside a Siemens Biograph Vision PET/CT is sold as 64- or 128-slice.',
 [IAEA, CANON, VISION, TOWNSEND04],
 'Draft ("64 to 320 detector rows") reworded: 64 to 320 is the range for current high-end stand-alone CT, but "slices" in product names is not always the number of physical rows (some 128-slice scanners sample 64 rows twice), and PET/CT scanners have been built with 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 64 and 128-slice CT (Townsend 2004 Table 2 lists 1 to 16 slices for the PET/CT designs of that year). IAEA handbook Table 11.2 and section 11.3.5: MDCT from 1998 with 4-64 active rows; volumetric CT from 2007 "with up to 320 active channels", "With the 320 detector row CT scanner, one single rotation allows for coverage of 160 mm". Canon page: "320 rows, 0.5 mm", "16 cm z-axis". Each row has roughly 800 to 1000 detector elements along the arc (IAEA).');
C('ct-slice-thickness', 'Claim 6: thinnest slices',
 'The thinnest slices on most current scanners are about 0.5 to 0.625 mm, and the newest photon-counting scanners reach 0.2 mm.',
 '0.5 to 0.625', 'mm',
 '0.5 mm (Canon), 0.6 mm (Siemens), 0.625 mm (GE); 0.2 mm on the Siemens NAEOTOM Alpha photon-counting scanner. Whole-body PET/CT images are usually reconstructed much thicker, for example 2 to 5 mm.',
 [CANON, VISION, HEADPH, NAEOTOM, IAEA, TOWNSEND04],
 'Draft confirmed; 0.2 mm figure added. Canon Aquilion ONE INSIGHT page: 320 rows, 0.5 mm. Siemens Biograph Vision page: example protocols with 128 x 0.6 mm and 32 x 1.2 mm collimation. GE Revolution scan with 0.625 mm slice thickness cited in PMC8693332. Siemens NAEOTOM Alpha page: "slice thickness of 0.2 mm", z-coverage 144 x 0.4 mm or 120 x 0.2 mm. IAEA: slice thickness "well below 1 mm" for MDCT. The example PET/CT scan in Townsend 2004 used 5 mm slices; the "2 to 5 mm" phrase is typical practice and should be checked against the PET/CT batch before use.');
C('ct-whole-body-scan-time', 'Claim 6: time for a whole-body CT pass',
 'The CT part of a PET/CT exam, from the head or neck down to the thighs, takes well under a minute, often 10 to 30 seconds.',
 '13', 's to cover 100 cm on a 16-slice PET/CT (2004 figure)',
 '13 s (16-slice) to 90 s (single-slice) per 100 cm on the PET/CT scanners of 2004; faster on current scanners. The PET part takes far longer, typically many minutes.',
 [TOWNSEND04, IAEA, FORCE],
 'Draft confirmed. Townsend et al. 2004 (J Nucl Med 45 suppl 1:4S-14S): "The time to scan an axial range of 100 cm varies from 13 s for a 16-slice system up to 90 s for a single-slice unit." IAEA handbook: MDCT makes it "possible to scan almost the entire body of an adult within one breath hold". Worked example (my arithmetic, not from a source): a 64 x 0.6 mm detector (38.4 mm) at pitch 1 and 0.5 s per turn moves ' + n.wholeBody_speed_mm_s + ' mm/s and covers 100 cm in ' + n.wholeBody_s + ' s. Siemens quotes up to 737 mm/s for its fastest stand-alone scanner. "Often 10 to 30 seconds" is my summary of these figures; no source read gives that range in those words.');
// 7
C('why-bone-stops-more-x-rays', 'Claim 7: why bone stops more X-rays',
 'Bone stops more X-rays than soft tissue for two reasons: it is nearly twice as dense, and about a third of its weight is calcium and phosphorus, heavier atoms that absorb X-ray photons outright far more readily than the carbon, oxygen and hydrogen of soft tissue.',
 'cortical bone: density 1.92 g/cm3; calcium 22.5% and phosphorus 10.3% by weight', '',
 'At 70 keV about ' + n.peB + ' percent of photon interactions in cortical bone are photoelectric absorption, compared with about ' + n.peW + ' percent in water; per centimeter, photoelectric absorption is about ' + Math.round(n.pePerCmRatio) + ' times more likely in bone than in water.',
 [TAB2, NIST+'bone.html', XCOM_MIX, XCOM_COMP, IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed and quantified. NIST Table 2 composition of cortical bone (ICRU-44): Ca 0.225, P 0.103, O 0.435, C 0.155, N 0.042, H 0.034 by weight; density 1.92 g/cm3. NIST XCOM at 70 keV: bone photoelectric 0.07729, incoherent (Compton) 0.1582, coherent (Rayleigh) 0.02157 cm2/g; water photoelectric 0.008969, incoherent 0.1734, coherent 0.01048 cm2/g. Shares: bone ' + n.peB + '% photoelectric / ' + n.incB + '% Compton / ' + n.cohB + '% Rayleigh; water ' + n.peW + '% / ' + n.incW + '% / ' + n.cohW + '%. Photoelectric per gram is ' + n.pePerGramRatio + ' times higher in bone, per cm ' + n.pePerCmRatio + ' times. NOTE: even in bone at 70 keV, Compton scattering is still the most common single interaction (' + n.incB + '%); photoelectric absorption becomes the majority only below roughly 50 keV. So say photoelectric absorption is "far more likely in bone than in soft tissue", not that it is the main interaction in bone at 70 keV. IAEA handbook: the photoelectric cross-section rises steeply with atomic number and falls steeply with energy; "scatter interactions are predominant for soft tissue, but the photoelectric interaction is predominant for bone over much of the diagnostic energy range".');
C('soft-tissue-mostly-scatters', 'Claim 7: soft tissue behaves like water and mostly scatters',
 'Soft tissues are mostly water, differ from water by only a few percent in how strongly they attenuate X-rays, and at CT energies about nine out of ten of their interactions with X-ray photons are Compton scattering.',
 n.incW, 'percent of interactions in water at 70 keV that are Compton scattering',
 'Water at 70 keV: about ' + n.incW + '% Compton scatter, ' + n.cohW + '% Rayleigh scatter, ' + n.peW + '% photoelectric absorption. Muscle and soft tissue are about ' + sig(100*(mu_.mu70/w.mu70-1),2) + ' to ' + sig(100*(s.mu70/w.mu70-1),2) + '% above water in mu; fat is about ' + sig(100*(1-f.mu70/w.mu70),2) + '% below.',
 [XCOM_COMP, XCOM_MIX, NIST+'tissue.html', NIST+'muscle.html', NIST+'adipose.html', IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed and quantified from NIST XCOM (see tables.interactions). mu at 70 keV: water ' + sig(w.mu70) + ', muscle ' + sig(mu_.mu70) + ', soft tissue ' + sig(s.mu70) + ', fat ' + sig(f.mu70) + ' per cm. This small spread (a few percent, i.e. a few tens of HU) is the reason CT needs precise measurement to tell soft tissues apart. At 511 keV more than 99.7% of interactions in water, soft tissue and bone are Compton scattering.');
// 8
C('three-fates-of-an-x-ray-photon', 'Claim 8: what can happen to one X-ray photon in the body',
 'Each X-ray photon entering the body does one of three things: it passes straight through and reaches the detector, it is absorbed by an atom and disappears (the photoelectric effect), or it bounces off an electron in a new direction (scattering).',
 'three outcomes: transmitted, absorbed, scattered', '',
 'Two kinds of scattering occur at CT energies: Compton scattering (the photon gives part of its energy to an electron; about ' + n.incW + '% of interactions in water at 70 keV) and Rayleigh scattering (the photon changes direction and keeps its energy; about ' + n.cohW + '%). A scattered photon can go on to scatter again, be absorbed, or leave the body.',
 [IAEA, XCOM_COMP],
 'Draft confirmed; Rayleigh (coherent) scattering added as a minor second kind of scatter so the site does not imply Compton is the only one. IAEA handbook chapter 2: photoelectric effect ("The incident photon disappears in the photoelectric interaction"), coherent (Rayleigh) scattering, Compton scattering; a scattered photon may "be followed by a third scatter, photoelectric absorption or no further interactions". Pair production cannot happen below 1022 keV, so it plays no part in CT or PET. The image is formed from the photons that pass straight through; scattered photons that still reach the detector add haze, which anti-scatter grids on the detector reduce.');
C('photons-do-not-slow-down', 'Claim 8: attenuation means fewer photons, not slower ones',
 'X-ray photons do not slow down in the body; they all travel at the speed of light, and tissue only reduces how many of them arrive, which is what attenuation means.',
 'speed unchanged; number reduced', '',
 'A Compton-scattered photon does lose energy, but it still travels at the speed of light: a 70 keV photon scattered through 90 degrees leaves with about ' + compton(70).after90deg_keV + ' keV, and a 511 keV photon with about ' + compton(511).after90deg_keV + ' keV.',
 [IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed. The Beer-Lambert law (IAEA Eq. 11.1) is a statement about the number (intensity) of unscattered photons. Lower photon energy means longer wavelength, not lower speed. The speed statement is standard physics rather than a quoted sentence from the handbook; for X-rays the refractive index of tissue differs from 1 by less than one part in 100,000, so "at the speed of light" is accurate for the site. Compton energies computed from E\' = E / (1 + (E/511 keV)(1 - cos angle)): 70 keV gives ' + compton(70).after90deg_keV + ' keV at 90 degrees and ' + compton(70).after180deg_keV + ' keV at 180 degrees; 511 keV gives ' + compton(511).after90deg_keV + ' keV and ' + compton(511).after180deg_keV + ' keV. GAME NOTE: if the site game shows photons slowing down in dense tissue, it should say plainly that this is a visual stand-in for "fewer photons get through", and that real photons never slow.');
