 'The size of the error grows with body size. Doctors still look at the uncorrected images as a cross-check, because errors in the CT (metal, contrast agent, breathing mismatch) can create false bright spots in the corrected ones.',
 [BAI03, SURESHBABU, BOELLAARD],
 'Draft confirmed. Bai, Kinahan et al. 2003 (J Nucl Med 44:1855-61): reconstruction without attenuation correction "illustrates the well-known artifacts of elevated tracer concentration in the lungs and skin"; in uniform regions such as the abdomen the uncorrected image is reduced "or even negative" toward the center of the object; "The well-known disadvantages of not performing attenuation correction are the inaccuracies in the uptake, shape, and location of lesions". Reason: photon pairs from deep in the body must cross more tissue, so fewer are detected; pairs from the skin, and pairs crossing the low-density lungs, are lost less. Sureshbabu and Mawlawi 2005 (J Nucl Med Technol 33:156-61) and the EANM guideline (Boellaard 2015) describe using non-corrected images to check for CT-caused artifacts.');
