C('ct-tube-voltage', 'Claim 1: CT X-ray tube voltage',
 'A CT scanner runs its X-ray tube at a peak voltage between about 70 and 150 kilovolts, and 120 kilovolts is the usual setting for adult body scans.',
 '120', 'kV (peak tube voltage, also written kVp)',
 '70 to 150 kV depending on scanner model; many scanners offer 80, 100, 120 and 140 kV. The IAEA handbook gives 120 to 140 kV as usual, with 80 to 100 kV for contrast studies and children.',
 [IAEA, FORCE, VISION, NAEOTOM],
 'Draft confirmed. Read: IAEA Diagnostic Radiology Physics handbook (2014), section 11.6 ("a relatively high tube voltage (120-140 kV) ... low tube voltage, in the range 80-100 kV") and section on dosimetry beam qualities (the standard CT quality RQT 9 is 120 kV). Siemens SOMATOM Force specification: "70 - 150 kV, in steps of 10 kV". Siemens Biograph Vision PET/CT: tube voltages 70, 80, 100, 120, 140 kV, so the CT inside a PET/CT may top out at 140 kV. Not every scanner offers 70 or 150 kV. "kVp" and "kV" are used interchangeably in the sources.');
C('ct-beam-energy-spectrum', 'Claim 1: X-ray beam energy spectrum and effective energy',
 'The beam is a mix of photon energies up to the tube voltage (up to 120 keV at 120 kV), and it behaves on average like a single-energy beam of roughly 55 to 70 keV.',
 '55 to 70', 'keV (effective energy at 120 kV)',
 'Measured effective energy at 120 kV, in air at the scanner center: 54.4 and 62.4 keV on two scanners (one study), 54.9 to 58.0 keV on two other scanners (another study). PET/CT papers use about 70 keV for the beam inside the patient.',
 [EFFE1, EFFE2, KINAHAN98, KINAHAN03, TOWNSEND04],
 'CORRECTED from the draft "about 60 to 70 keV": measured effective energies at 120 kVp are often below 60 keV (54.4 keV and 62.4 keV in PMC5966206; 54.87 to 57.97 keV, mean 56.47, in PMC7851788). The PET/CT literature (Kinahan 1998 abstract: "effective CT photon energy of 70 keV"; Kinahan 2003: "approximately 70 keV"; Townsend 2004: "mean photon energy of 70 keV") uses 70 keV; the beam gets "harder" (higher average energy) as low-energy photons are filtered out by the patient. "Effective energy" (single energy with the same half-value layer) and "mean energy" (average of the spectrum) are not the same quantity; sources use both loosely. Kinahan 1998 describes the CT photons as spanning 40 keV through 140 keV. The site tables use 70 keV to match the PET/CT convention; say "about 70 keV" rather than implying it is exact.');
// 2
C('beer-lambert-law', 'Claim 2: Beer-Lambert law',
 'The number of photons that get straight through a material falls off exponentially with thickness: I = I0 x exp(-mu x x), where mu is the linear attenuation coefficient of the material and x is the thickness.',
 'I = I0 * exp(-mu * x)', '',
 'Exact only for a narrow beam of a single energy passing through one uniform material; for a path through several tissues the exponent is the sum (integral) of mu along the path.',
 [IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed. IAEA handbook Eq. 11.1 ("Beer\'s law": I(x) = I0 e^(-mu x)) and Eq. 11.2 for a path through varying tissue. The handbook notes that the law describes only the primary (unscattered) beam and that a real CT beam has many energies, which is the cause of the beam-hardening artifact. The site may call it Beer\'s law, the Beer-Lambert law or the exponential attenuation law; all are in use.');
