 'See tables.attenuation. Body tissues vary from person to person; lung varies most (roughly 0.1 to 0.5 g/cm3 with breathing), and bone ranges from spongy to compact.',
 [TAB4, TAB2, ...M.map(m => NIST+m.page), XCOM_MIX],
 'Mass attenuation coefficients at 60, 80, 500 and 600 keV were read from the NIST pages and densities from NIST Table 2 (air 1.205E-03, adipose 0.950, water 1.000, soft tissue ICRU-44 1.060, skeletal muscle 1.050, cortical bone 1.920, lung tissue 1.050 g/cm3). mu at 70 and 511 keV by log-log interpolation as the task specified. CHECK: NIST XCOM run directly at 70 keV with the same compositions gives slightly lower values, because the curve between 60 and 80 keV is not a straight line on log-log axes: water 0.1929 vs 0.1937 cm2/g (0.4% lower), soft tissue 0.1916 vs 0.1924, fat 0.1875 vs 0.1879, cortical bone 0.2570 vs 0.2616 (1.8% lower). The XCOM values are in the table as xcom_* columns and are the better numbers if the site shows more than two significant figures; at 511 keV the two methods agree to 4 figures. DOUBT: the inflated-lung row uses the NIST lung-tissue mu/rho (tabulated for solid lung tissue, 1.05 g/cm3) at an assumed density of 0.26 g/cm3, as the task instructed. 0.26 g/cm3 is the inflated-lung value usually attributed to ICRU Report 44, but I could not open a primary source that states it; the site should say "inflated lung, assumed density 0.26 g/cm3". The computed HU for that row (' + Math.round(l.hu70) + ') sits inside the IAEA lung range (-950 to -600). typicalHU ranges are from claim 5 sources, not from NIST. computedHU_monoenergetic shows what a single-energy beam would give; real scanners differ.');
