 'Draft formula kept as an accepted variant, but the form with mu_water alone in the denominator is the one given by the IAEA handbook (Eq. 11.4), which then states HU(air) = -1000 by taking mu_air = 0. With the NIST values at 70 keV the IAEA form gives air = ' + n.airHU_iaeaForm + ' HU, so "air is -1000" is a rounded convention under that form and exact under the two-point form. Both forms are true to within 1.1 HU. The scale is named for Godfrey Hounsfield; in his 1979 Nobel lecture he describes the scale running "from air (-1000) at the bottom ... to bone at the top", with water chosen as zero.');
