C('hounsfield-spongy-bone', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, spongy (cancellous) bone',
 'Spongy bone, such as the inside of a vertebra, varies widely with age and bone health, from under +100 HU to +300 HU or more.',
 '+100 to +300', 'HU', 'In a study of 1867 adults, a first-lumbar-vertebra reading of 160 HU or less suggested osteoporosis and readings above 200 HU made it very unlikely; individual readings as low as 20 HU were shown.',
 [PICKHARDT, IAEA],
 'CORRECTED: the draft range "+300 to +400" is widely repeated on the web but I found no primary source for it, and measured vertebral spongy bone is usually lower. Pickhardt et al. 2013 (Ann Intern Med; PMC3736840): L1 trabecular attenuation of 160 HU or less was 90% sensitive, and 110 HU more than 90% specific, for osteoporosis; negative predictive value 99% above 200 HU; example patients at 20, 93 and 148 HU. Spongy bone in young adults and at other sites can be denser; the IAEA table gives only "compact bone" starting at +300. Recommend the site avoid a single narrow range for spongy bone.');
C('hounsfield-cortical-bone', 'Claim 5: Hounsfield units, compact (cortical) bone',
 'Dense compact bone measures from several hundred HU to +2000 HU or more, typically around +1000 to +1500 HU.',
 '+1000', 'HU', '+300 to +2500 (IAEA). Depends strongly on tube voltage: computed for reference cortical bone, +' + Math.round(b.hu80) + ' at 80 keV, +' + Math.round(b.hu70) + ' at 70 keV and +' + Math.round(b.hu60) + ' at 60 keV.',
 [IAEA, HEADPH, NIST+'bone.html'],
 'Draft (+500 up to about +1900 or more) is consistent with the sources; range replaced with the IAEA handbook Table 11.1 entry: Compact bone +1000 (+300 to +2500). A 2021 phantom paper lists skull bone at about 1500 HU at 120 kV on a GE Revolution scanner. The IAEA handbook notes that HU of high-atomic-number materials such as bone change most with tube voltage.');
// 6
C('ct-gantry-rotation-time', 'Claim 6: gantry rotation speed',
 'Inside the ring, the X-ray tube and the detector opposite it spin around the patient about two to four times every second.',
 '0.25 to 0.5', 's per rotation',
 'Fastest current models: 0.23 s (GE Revolution Apex), 0.24 s (Canon Aquilion ONE INSIGHT Edition, optional), 0.25 s (Siemens SOMATOM Force). The CT inside a Siemens Biograph Vision PET/CT: 0.33 s, with 0.30 and 0.28 s optional. Slower settings of 0.5 to 1 s are also used.',
 [IAEA, FORCE, GEPR, FDA_GE, CANON, VISION],
 'Draft confirmed with detail added. 0.5 s = 2 turns per second; 0.33 s = 3; 0.25 s = 4; 0.23 s = ' + n.rps['0.23'] + '. IAEA handbook: multi-detector scanners brought rotation time to 0.3-0.4 s, and "forces of several tens of g arise for rotation times of the order of 0.25 s". Manufacturer pages read 2026-11-18; GE press release calls 0.23 s "the world\'s fastest gantry speed", a marketing claim the site should not repeat as fact. The FDA 510(k) summary K213715 confirms the 0.23 s/rotation option. The fastest speeds are options used mainly for heart scans; a PET/CT body scan does not need them.');
C('ct-detector-rows', 'Claim 6: number of detector rows',
 'Instead of one row of detector elements, a modern CT scanner has many rows side by side, commonly 64 and up to 320, so each turn records a band of the body rather than a single thin slice.',
 '64', 'detector rows (common); up to 320',
 '4 to 64 rows in the multi-detector scanners introduced from 1998; up to 320 rows of 0.5 mm, covering 16 cm per turn, on wide-detector scanners. The CT inside a Siemens Biograph Vision PET/CT is sold as 64- or 128-slice.',
 [IAEA, CANON, VISION, TOWNSEND04],
 'Draft ("64 to 320 detector rows") reworded: 64 to 320 is the range for current high-end stand-alone CT, but "slices" in product names is not always the number of physical rows (some 128-slice scanners sample 64 rows twice), and PET/CT scanners have been built with 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 64 and 128-slice CT (Townsend 2004 Table 2 lists 1 to 16 slices for the PET/CT designs of that year). IAEA handbook Table 11.2 and section 11.3.5: MDCT from 1998 with 4-64 active rows; volumetric CT from 2007 "with up to 320 active channels", "With the 320 detector row CT scanner, one single rotation allows for coverage of 160 mm". Canon page: "320 rows, 0.5 mm", "16 cm z-axis". Each row has roughly 800 to 1000 detector elements along the arc (IAEA).');
C('ct-slice-thickness', 'Claim 6: thinnest slices',
 'The thinnest slices on most current scanners are about 0.5 to 0.625 mm, and the newest photon-counting scanners reach 0.2 mm.',
 '0.5 to 0.625', 'mm',
 '0.5 mm (Canon), 0.6 mm (Siemens), 0.625 mm (GE); 0.2 mm on the Siemens NAEOTOM Alpha photon-counting scanner. Whole-body PET/CT images are usually reconstructed much thicker, for example 2 to 5 mm.',
 [CANON, VISION, HEADPH, NAEOTOM, IAEA, TOWNSEND04],
 'Draft confirmed; 0.2 mm figure added. Canon Aquilion ONE INSIGHT page: 320 rows, 0.5 mm. Siemens Biograph Vision page: example protocols with 128 x 0.6 mm and 32 x 1.2 mm collimation. GE Revolution scan with 0.625 mm slice thickness cited in PMC8693332. Siemens NAEOTOM Alpha page: "slice thickness of 0.2 mm", z-coverage 144 x 0.4 mm or 120 x 0.2 mm. IAEA: slice thickness "well below 1 mm" for MDCT. The example PET/CT scan in Townsend 2004 used 5 mm slices; the "2 to 5 mm" phrase is typical practice and should be checked against the PET/CT batch before use.');
C('ct-whole-body-scan-time', 'Claim 6: time for a whole-body CT pass',
 'The CT part of a PET/CT exam, from the head or neck down to the thighs, takes well under a minute, often 10 to 30 seconds.',
 '13', 's to cover 100 cm on a 16-slice PET/CT (2004 figure)',
 '13 s (16-slice) to 90 s (single-slice) per 100 cm on the PET/CT scanners of 2004; faster on current scanners. The PET part takes far longer, typically many minutes.',
 [TOWNSEND04, IAEA, FORCE],
 'Draft confirmed. Townsend et al. 2004 (J Nucl Med 45 suppl 1:4S-14S): "The time to scan an axial range of 100 cm varies from 13 s for a 16-slice system up to 90 s for a single-slice unit." IAEA handbook: MDCT makes it "possible to scan almost the entire body of an adult within one breath hold". Worked example (my arithmetic, not from a source): a 64 x 0.6 mm detector (38.4 mm) at pitch 1 and 0.5 s per turn moves ' + n.wholeBody_speed_mm_s + ' mm/s and covers 100 cm in ' + n.wholeBody_s + ' s. Siemens quotes up to 737 mm/s for its fastest stand-alone scanner. "Often 10 to 30 seconds" is my summary of these figures; no source read gives that range in those words.');
// 7
C('why-bone-stops-more-x-rays', 'Claim 7: why bone stops more X-rays',
 'Bone stops more X-rays than soft tissue for two reasons: it is nearly twice as dense, and about a third of its weight is calcium and phosphorus, heavier atoms that absorb X-ray photons outright far more readily than the carbon, oxygen and hydrogen of soft tissue.',
 'cortical bone: density 1.92 g/cm3; calcium 22.5% and phosphorus 10.3% by weight', '',
 'At 70 keV about ' + n.peB + ' percent of photon interactions in cortical bone are photoelectric absorption, compared with about ' + n.peW + ' percent in water; per centimeter, photoelectric absorption is about ' + Math.round(n.pePerCmRatio) + ' times more likely in bone than in water.',
 [TAB2, NIST+'bone.html', XCOM_MIX, XCOM_COMP, IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed and quantified. NIST Table 2 composition of cortical bone (ICRU-44): Ca 0.225, P 0.103, O 0.435, C 0.155, N 0.042, H 0.034 by weight; density 1.92 g/cm3. NIST XCOM at 70 keV: bone photoelectric 0.07729, incoherent (Compton) 0.1582, coherent (Rayleigh) 0.02157 cm2/g; water photoelectric 0.008969, incoherent 0.1734, coherent 0.01048 cm2/g. Shares: bone ' + n.peB + '% photoelectric / ' + n.incB + '% Compton / ' + n.cohB + '% Rayleigh; water ' + n.peW + '% / ' + n.incW + '% / ' + n.cohW + '%. Photoelectric per gram is ' + n.pePerGramRatio + ' times higher in bone, per cm ' + n.pePerCmRatio + ' times. NOTE: even in bone at 70 keV, Compton scattering is still the most common single interaction (' + n.incB + '%); photoelectric absorption becomes the majority only below roughly 50 keV. So say photoelectric absorption is "far more likely in bone than in soft tissue", not that it is the main interaction in bone at 70 keV. IAEA handbook: the photoelectric cross-section rises steeply with atomic number and falls steeply with energy; "scatter interactions are predominant for soft tissue, but the photoelectric interaction is predominant for bone over much of the diagnostic energy range".');
C('soft-tissue-mostly-scatters', 'Claim 7: soft tissue behaves like water and mostly scatters',
 'Soft tissues are mostly water, differ from water by only a few percent in how strongly they attenuate X-rays, and at CT energies about nine out of ten of their interactions with X-ray photons are Compton scattering.',
 n.incW, 'percent of interactions in water at 70 keV that are Compton scattering',
 'Water at 70 keV: about ' + n.incW + '% Compton scatter, ' + n.cohW + '% Rayleigh scatter, ' + n.peW + '% photoelectric absorption. Muscle and soft tissue are about ' + sig(100*(mu_.mu70/w.mu70-1),2) + ' to ' + sig(100*(s.mu70/w.mu70-1),2) + '% above water in mu; fat is about ' + sig(100*(1-f.mu70/w.mu70),2) + '% below.',
 [XCOM_COMP, XCOM_MIX, NIST+'tissue.html', NIST+'muscle.html', NIST+'adipose.html', IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed and quantified from NIST XCOM (see tables.interactions). mu at 70 keV: water ' + sig(w.mu70) + ', muscle ' + sig(mu_.mu70) + ', soft tissue ' + sig(s.mu70) + ', fat ' + sig(f.mu70) + ' per cm. This small spread (a few percent, i.e. a few tens of HU) is the reason CT needs precise measurement to tell soft tissues apart. At 511 keV more than 99.7% of interactions in water, soft tissue and bone are Compton scattering.');
// 8
C('three-fates-of-an-x-ray-photon', 'Claim 8: what can happen to one X-ray photon in the body',
 'Each X-ray photon entering the body does one of three things: it passes straight through and reaches the detector, it is absorbed by an atom and disappears (the photoelectric effect), or it bounces off an electron in a new direction (scattering).',
 'three outcomes: transmitted, absorbed, scattered', '',
 'Two kinds of scattering occur at CT energies: Compton scattering (the photon gives part of its energy to an electron; about ' + n.incW + '% of interactions in water at 70 keV) and Rayleigh scattering (the photon changes direction and keeps its energy; about ' + n.cohW + '%). A scattered photon can go on to scatter again, be absorbed, or leave the body.',
 [IAEA, XCOM_COMP],
 'Draft confirmed; Rayleigh (coherent) scattering added as a minor second kind of scatter so the site does not imply Compton is the only one. IAEA handbook chapter 2: photoelectric effect ("The incident photon disappears in the photoelectric interaction"), coherent (Rayleigh) scattering, Compton scattering; a scattered photon may "be followed by a third scatter, photoelectric absorption or no further interactions". Pair production cannot happen below 1022 keV, so it plays no part in CT or PET. The image is formed from the photons that pass straight through; scattered photons that still reach the detector add haze, which anti-scatter grids on the detector reduce.');
C('photons-do-not-slow-down', 'Claim 8: attenuation means fewer photons, not slower ones',
 'X-ray photons do not slow down in the body; they all travel at the speed of light, and tissue only reduces how many of them arrive, which is what attenuation means.',
 'speed unchanged; number reduced', '',
 'A Compton-scattered photon does lose energy, but it still travels at the speed of light: a 70 keV photon scattered through 90 degrees leaves with about ' + compton(70).after90deg_keV + ' keV, and a 511 keV photon with about ' + compton(511).after90deg_keV + ' keV.',
 [IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed. The Beer-Lambert law (IAEA Eq. 11.1) is a statement about the number (intensity) of unscattered photons. Lower photon energy means longer wavelength, not lower speed. The speed statement is standard physics rather than a quoted sentence from the handbook; for X-rays the refractive index of tissue differs from 1 by less than one part in 100,000, so "at the speed of light" is accurate for the site. Compton energies computed from E\' = E / (1 + (E/511 keV)(1 - cos angle)): 70 keV gives ' + compton(70).after90deg_keV + ' keV at 90 degrees and ' + compton(70).after180deg_keV + ' keV at 180 degrees; 511 keV gives ' + compton(511).after90deg_keV + ' keV and ' + compton(511).after180deg_keV + ' keV. GAME NOTE: if the site game shows photons slowing down in dense tissue, it should say plainly that this is a visual stand-in for "fewer photons get through", and that real photons never slow.');
// 9
C('radon-transform', 'Claim 9: image reconstruction, the Radon transform',
 'The mathematics behind CT was worked out in 1917 by the mathematician Johann Radon, who showed that a two-dimensional pattern can be recovered exactly from the sums measured along all the straight lines crossing it; the set of those line sums is now called the Radon transform.',
 '1917', 'year',
 'Radon\'s paper was unknown to the people who built CT: Allan Cormack solved the problem independently and published in 1963 and 1964, and wrote that he learned of Radon\'s work only in the early 1970s.',
 [CORMACK, NOBELPRESS, IAEA],
 'Draft confirmed. Cormack Nobel lecture (1979): "Only then [the early seventies] did I learn of Radon\'s work on the line integral problem", reference 3: Radon, J., Ber. Sachs. Akad. Wiss. 69, 262 (1917); "Radon provided an affirmative answer and a formula". Nobel press release 1979: Cormack "published his analysis of the problem in two scientific publications in 1963 and 1964" and "was not aware then that the key mathematical problems had been considered earlier". IAEA handbook section 11.4.2 uses "Radon space" and "2-D Radon transform" for CT projection data. Radon\'s nationality (Austrian) is from general knowledge and was not checked in a source read for this batch, so it is left out of the statement.');
C('filtered-back-projection', 'Claim 9: image reconstruction, filtered back-projection',
 'For about four decades the standard way to turn the measurements into a picture was filtered back-projection: each measured profile is sharpened with a "ramp" filter and then smeared back across the image along the direction it was measured from, and the smears from all angles add up to the cross-section.',
 'about 40', 'years as the standard method (1970s to 2010s)',
 'Without the filter step, simple back-projection gives a strongly blurred image. The ramp filter is also called the Ram-Lak (Ramachandran-Lakshminarayanan) filter; scanners offer modified versions tuned for soft tissue or bone.',
 [IAEA, KOETZIER, WILLEMINK],
 'Draft confirmed. Koetzier et al. 2023 (Radiology 306:e221257): "Filtered back projection (FBP) has been the standard CT image reconstruction method for 4 decades." Willemink and Noel 2019 (Eur Radiol 29:2185-95): "FBP was the method of choice for decades". IAEA handbook section 11.4: simple back-projection "yields a strongly blurred image"; the filter is "a ramp filter in the frequency domain ... Ramachandran-Lakshminarayanan filter, also called the Ram-Lak or ramp filter".');
C('first-ct-used-iterative-reconstruction', 'Claim 9: image reconstruction, history',
 'The first CT scanners of the early 1970s built their images with a step-by-step (iterative) method, but the computers of the time were too slow for it and filtered back-projection soon replaced it.',
 'early 1970s', '',
 'The first clinical scan took about 5 minutes to acquire and about as long to reconstruct, at 80 x 80 pixels; today images are usually 512 x 512 pixels.',
 [WILLEMINK],
 'ADDED nuance: the draft says FBP "was the standard method for decades", which is true, but it was not the original method. Willemink and Noel 2019: "The first CT scanners in the early 1970s already used iterative reconstruction algorithms"; "Originally, CT images were reconstructed with an iterative method called algebraic reconstruction technique (ART). Due to lack of computational power, this technique was quickly replaced by simple analytic methods such as filtered back projection"; "The first clinical CT scan took about 5 min, and image reconstruction took approximately the same time ... only 80 x 80 pixels".');
C('iterative-reconstruction-return', 'Claim 9: image reconstruction, iterative methods',
 'Since about 2009, faster computers have let scanner makers bring back iterative reconstruction, which repeatedly adjusts the image until it agrees with the measurements and gives cleaner pictures at lower radiation dose; it is now common.',
 '2009', 'year (approximate start of routine clinical use)',
 'Within a few years of 2009 all major CT makers offered iterative reconstruction. Types range from "hybrid" methods that mainly reduce noise to full model-based methods.',
 [WILLEMINK, IAEA, KOETZIER],
 'Draft confirmed. Willemink and Noel 2019: "it took until 2009 for the first iterative reconstruction algorithms to come commercially available"; "In 2009, the first IR algorithm called IRIS (iterative reconstruction in image space, Siemens Healthineers) received FDA clearance". DOUBT on the "first": other accounts date GE\'s ASiR to 2008, which I did not verify; the statement therefore says "since about 2009" and names no first product. IAEA handbook (2014): "Iterative (statistical) reconstructions are now commonly used in CT."');
C('deep-learning-reconstruction', 'Claim 9: image reconstruction, deep learning',
 'The newest method, deep-learning reconstruction, uses a neural network trained on many pairs of noisy and clean scans to produce low-noise images; the first such products for CT were cleared by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration in 2019.',
 '2019', 'year of first FDA clearances',
 'By 2023, three CT makers (GE, Canon, Philips) and two independent software companies had FDA-cleared deep-learning products; a 2023 review reports radiation dose reductions of up to 71 percent in studies.',
 [KOETZIER],
 'Draft confirmed and dated. Koetzier et al. 2023: "In 2019, TrueFidelity (GE Healthcare) became the first DLR technique approved by the FDA"; "The second DLR algorithm approved by the FDA was ... AiCE, Canon Medical Systems, in 2019"; Philips Precise Image in 2022; two image-denoising products (ClariCT.AI, PixelShine) cleared in 2019. The statement says "the first such products ... in 2019", which is true whichever product is counted first. The review uses "approved"; for these devices the FDA term is "cleared" (510(k)). The 71% dose figure is from the review abstract as returned by search and applies to selected studies, not to every scan; omit it if space is short.');
// 10
C('ct-based-attenuation-correction', 'Claim 10: CT-based attenuation correction in PET/CT',
 'In a PET/CT scanner the CT image does a second job: it is converted into a map of how strongly each part of the body attenuates 511 keV photons, and that map is used to correct the PET data for the photon pairs that were absorbed or scattered inside the patient.',
 'attenuation correction', '',
 'Before PET/CT, the map came from a separate transmission scan with a rotating radioactive source, which took several minutes per bed position and was noisy; using CT cut whole-body scan time by up to 40 percent.',
 [TOWNSEND04, KINAHAN03, SURESHBABU, BOELLAARD],
 'Draft confirmed. Townsend et al. 2004: "use of CT images for attenuation correction of the PET emission data, eliminating the need for a separate, lengthy PET transmission scan ... not only reduces whole-body scan times by up to 40% but also provides essentially noiseless ACFs". Kinahan et al. 2003 (Semin Nucl Med 33:166-79) abstract lists the trade-offs and the error sources: breathing mismatch between CT and PET, contrast agents, metal implants, and arms in the field of view. The EANM FDG PET/CT guideline (Boellaard 2015) recommends looking at images with and without attenuation correction to spot CT-caused artifacts.');
C('ct-to-511-kev-conversion', 'Claim 10: converting CT numbers to 511 keV attenuation',
 'Because CT measures attenuation at about 70 keV and PET needs it at 511 keV, the scanner rescales every pixel, using one conversion line for air, lung and soft tissue and a different, flatter line for bone.',
 'mu(511 keV) = 9.6e-5 x (HU + 1000) per cm below about 50 HU', 'per cm',
 'This two-line ("bilinear") conversion gives 0.096 per cm for water (0 HU) and 0 for air. Above the break point the slope is smaller and depends on the CT tube voltage. The ratio mu(511 keV)/mu(70 keV) is about ' + sig(n.scaleWater,2) + ' for water and about ' + sig(n.scaleBone,2) + ' for dense bone.',
 [CARNEY06, BURGER02, TOWNSEND04, SURESHBABU, NIST+'water.html', NIST+'bone.html'],
 'Draft confirmed with one correction of attribution (see kinahan-1998-reference): the bilinear method is described by Burger et al. 2002 for the GE Discovery LS ("uses a bilinear function based on the attenuation of water and cortical bone at the CT and PET energies") and by Carney et al. 2006 ("9.6 x 10^-5 x (HU + 1000) below a threshold of approximately 50 HU and a(HU + 1000) + b above the threshold, where a and b ... depend on the kVp setting"). Break points and slopes differ between manufacturers. Check: Carney\'s line gives ' + n.carneyLung + ' per cm for the inflated-lung row (HU ' + Math.round(l.hu70) + '), against ' + sig(l.mu511,3) + ' computed from NIST. Scale ratios computed from NIST XCOM: water 0.09598/0.1929 = ' + n.scaleWater + '; cortical bone 0.08938/0.2570 = ' + n.scaleBone + '. Contrast agents and metal break the conversion because they are bright on CT for a reason (high atomic number) that matters little at 511 keV.');
C('kinahan-1998-reference', 'Claim 10: key reference, Kinahan et al. 1998',
 'The method was first demonstrated in a 1998 paper in Medical Physics by Paul Kinahan, David Townsend, Thomas Beyer and Donald Sashin, who used CT scans of test objects and of people to correct PET data and concluded that the approach was feasible for a combined PET/CT scanner.',
 'Kinahan PE, Townsend DW, Beyer T, Sashin D. Attenuation correction for a combined 3D PET/CT scanner. Med Phys. 1998;25(10):2046-2053. doi:10.1118/1.598392', '',
 'Published October 1998; PubMed ID 9800714. The data came from separate CT and PET scanners, with the images aligned by software.',
 [KINAHAN98, TOWNSEND04],
 'Reference CONFIRMED (authors, journal, year, volume 25, issue 10, pages 2046-53, DOI). CORRECTION to the draft: this paper did not propose "bilinear scaling". Its abstract compares three methods, "segmentation, scaling, and our proposed hybrid segmentation/scaling method", converting "from an effective CT photon energy of 70 keV to the PET photon energy of 511 keV"; the hybrid method uses a threshold to separate bone from non-bone and applies a different scale factor to each (Townsend 2004). The bilinear function used on later scanners is a close relative (Burger 2002, Carney 2006). First-author affiliation in PubMed: Department of Radiology, University of Pittsburgh. "First demonstrated" is supported by the abstract\'s "proof of principle" wording for CT-based correction of 3D PET; X-ray-based attenuation correction had earlier been explored for SPECT/CT by Hasegawa\'s group, so do not write "first use of CT for attenuation correction in nuclear medicine". Safer wording if in doubt: "A 1998 paper ... showed that it works".');
C('pet-without-attenuation-correction', 'Claim 10: what an uncorrected PET image looks like',
 'Without attenuation correction a PET image is misleading in a recognizable way: the skin and the lungs look too bright, and the deep center of the body looks too dim.',
 'skin and lungs falsely bright; deep tissue falsely dim', '',
 'The size of the error grows with body size. Doctors still look at the uncorrected images as a cross-check, because errors in the CT (metal, contrast agent, breathing mismatch) can create false bright spots in the corrected ones.',
 [BAI03, SURESHBABU, BOELLAARD],
 'Draft confirmed. Bai, Kinahan et al. 2003 (J Nucl Med 44:1855-61): reconstruction without attenuation correction "illustrates the well-known artifacts of elevated tracer concentration in the lungs and skin"; in uniform regions such as the abdomen the uncorrected image is reduced "or even negative" toward the center of the object; "The well-known disadvantages of not performing attenuation correction are the inaccuracies in the uptake, shape, and location of lesions". Reason: photon pairs from deep in the body must cross more tissue, so fewer are detected; pairs from the skin, and pairs crossing the low-density lungs, are lost less. Sureshbabu and Mawlawi 2005 (J Nucl Med Technol 33:156-61) and the EANM guideline (Boellaard 2015) describe using non-corrected images to check for CT-caused artifacts.');
C('photon-pair-loss-in-the-body', 'Claim 10: size of the attenuation effect at 511 keV',
 'A PET event is counted only if both 511 keV photons escape the body, and along a line through 30 cm of tissue only about 1 pair in 18 does, so the correction is large.',
 sig(T(w.mu511,30),2), 'percent of photon pairs surviving a 30 cm path in water',
 'Survival for a total path in water of 10 / 20 / 30 / 40 cm: ' + pairSurvival.map(p => p.pctOfPhotonPairsDetectable).join(' / ') + ' percent (correction factors ' + pairSurvival.map(p => p.correctionFactor).join(' / ') + '). The loss depends on the total tissue thickness along the line, not on where along the line the pair started.',
 [NIST+'water.html', BURGER02, BAI03],
 'Computed here, not quoted: pair survival = exp(-mu x D) with mu(water, 511 keV) = ' + sig(w.mu511) + ' per cm and D the total path length through the body along the line joining the two detectors (each photon survives with exp(-mu x its own path), and the two paths add up to D). 30 cm: exp(-' + sig(w.mu511) + ' x 30) = ' + sig(Math.exp(-w.mu511*30),3) + ', i.e. 1 in ' + sig(Math.exp(w.mu511*30),3) + '. Real bodies are not pure water (lungs attenuate less, bone more), and 30 cm is a typical adult torso width chosen for illustration. This is added background for the site text; the draft did not include a number.');

const out = {
  batch: 'ct-physics',
  verifiedOn: '2026-11-18',
  claims,
  tables: {
    attenuation,
    transmission,
    hounsfield,
    interactions,
    pairSurvival511keV: pairSurvival,
    tableNotes: {
      attenuation: 'massAtt_* columns are cm2/g exactly as printed in the NIST X-ray mass attenuation tables (Hubbell and Seltzer, NIST Standard Reference Database 126). mu_70keV_per_cm and mu_511keV_per_cm = density x log-log interpolation between 60 and 80 keV and between 500 and 600 keV. xcom_* columns are from the NIST XCOM database run at exactly 70 and 511 keV with the NIST Table 2 compositions; they reproduce the tabulated 60, 80 and 500 keV values exactly and are 0.2 to 1.8% lower than the interpolated values at 70 keV. Densities from NIST Table 2 except inflated lung (assumed 0.26 g/cm3). typicalHU are literature ranges (see tables.hounsfield).',
      transmission: 'Percent of 70 keV photons that cross the stated thickness without being absorbed or scattered = 100 x exp(-mu x thickness), using mu_70keV_per_cm from tables.attenuation. xcomCheck_pctThrough repeats the calculation with the XCOM mu. at511keV_pctThrough is the same for a single 511 keV photon.',
      interactions: 'Share of each interaction type, from NIST XCOM partial coefficients (photoelectric, incoherent = Compton, coherent = Rayleigh).',
      hounsfield: 'typicalHU = [low, high] range recommended for the site, with the evidence in "basis".',
      pairSurvival511keV: 'Fraction of annihilation photon pairs for which both photons escape a water path of the given total length.',
    },
  },
};
fs.writeFileSync(process.argv[2], JSON.stringify(out, null, 2), 'utf8');
console.log('claims', claims.length);
console.log(JSON.stringify(n, null, 1));
console.table(attenuation.map(r => ({ m: r.material.slice(0,18), rho: r.density_g_cm3, mu70: r.mu_70keV_per_cm, mu511: r.mu_511keV_per_cm, x70: r.xcom_mu_70keV_per_cm, x511: r.xcom_mu_511keV_per_cm, HU70: r.computedHU_monoenergetic.at70keV })));
console.table(transmission.map(r => ({ m: r.material.slice(0,18), c1: r.pctThrough_1cm, c5: r.pctThrough_5cm, c10: r.pctThrough_10cm, c20: r.pctThrough_20cm })));
console.table(interactions.map(r => ({ m: r.material.slice(0,18), ...r.at70keV_pct })));
EOF
node "$D/scripts/build_draft.js" "$D/draft.json"

